y in her NEXT class. It MUST be rooted in THIS exact lesson AND fuse her own value (from her Q3 answer + strengths) with THE TARGET below. Phrase it as an implementation intention anchored to next class (\"Next class, when [trigger], [do X]\") — but respect the gender-neutral rule above (imperative, not a gendered \"you will\"). Max ~32 words. Vivid and classroom-specific — name the actual materials/concept from THIS lesson. NOT generic.\n\nAlso return \"highlights\": an array of 2–4 short English keyword phrases that appear verbatim in \"action\" (concrete nouns) to visually emphasise. And \"lesson_label\": a 2–4 word English subject·topic label.\n\nSession (framework: FICO):\nTHE TARGET (fixed — do NOT choose a different area): indicator B6 \"Differentiation / Catering to Learning Levels\". Why it is the next step: No evidence of distinct tasks or supports was observed (score 0); adding even one simple scaffold and one extension will increase learning access for low and high performers. The scorer's suggested move: \"Give two versions of the final task: (A) identify/write the fraction for a shaded shape, (B) create and label a shaded fraction to challenge fast finishers.\". Your \"action\" is about THIS indicator only — ONE move, not a list of moves.\n\n- Her strengths: Use of multiple representations\n- Growth areas: Differentiation / Catering to Learning Levels (B6) — All students received the same tasks and instructions; no easier or extension tasks or named targeted supports were provided. Strategy: Plan two-tiered exit tasks as suggested in the original focus: (A) identify/write the fraction for a shaded shape, (B) create and label a shaded fraction to challenge fast finishers. || Mathematical Reasoning & Wait-Time (F4/F5) — Teacher asked occasional 'why?' but did not press for full reasoning or provide think time. Strategy: After asking 'why?', pause for 8–10 seconds; invite a different student to rephrase the prior student's reason. || Student Agency & Voic